    "case_id": "602",
    "case_summary": "A 31-year-old African American man originally from the Togolese Republic presented with right foot/ankle swelling and pain. He reported associated fevers that began the morning before presenting. Medical history includes a condition diagnosed at 8 years old.\n\nPatient was tachycardic, diaphoretic, and febrile. Several areas of skin over his face, chest, and abdomen showed hardened plaque and depigmentation. Over the upper extremities, hardened nodules of varying sizes were noted, along with larger hardened masses that were fixed and nontender to palpation. The right foot/ankle was fluctuant and tender to palpation.\n\nLaboratory Results:\n- Normal renal function\n- Leukocytosis (WBC count: 18.7 × 10³/UL)\n- Elevated total creatine kinase (1947 U/L)\n- Normal corrected calcium (9.62 mg/dL)\n- Depressed phosphorus (1.7 mg/dL)\n- Normal lactic acid (1.7 mmol/L) and magnesium (1.9 mg/dL)\n\nImaging Studies:\n- X-ray sagittal view of right lower extremity\n  Image Description: Showed soft tissue calcifications unrelated to bone or cartilage, negative for osteomyelitis or subcutaneous gas\n- CT scan of right lower extremity\n  Image Description: Sagittal view at the level of the cuboid and fibular malleolus showing soft tissue calcifications\n\nPathology:\n- Findings confirmed calcified tissue with inflamed stroma and necrosis",
